  "scenes": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "narration": "Welcome back! Today, we are learning about operators. These are the symbols that allow us to perform math and logic in our Java programs. Let's get started.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TitleCard",
        "props": {
          "title": "Operators and Expressions",
          "subtitle": "Doing Math in Java",
          "badge": "DAY 5 • JAVA",
          "tags": [
            "Java",
            "Operators",
            "Beginner"
          ]
        },
        "chapter": "M1 Setup and Basics"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "narration": "Imagine you are building a simple shopping app. You have the price of an apple and a banana. How do you find the total? You need a way to tell Java to add them.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "SummaryList",
        "props": {
          "title": "The Shopping Problem",
          "items": [
            "Apple Price: 50",
            "Banana Price: 30",
            "How to get the total?",
            "We need an addition operator"
          ]
        },
        "keyIdea": "Operators perform actions"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "narration": "An operator is like a button on a calculator. The plus sign, minus sign, and multiply sign are all operators. They take values and produce a new result.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "ArchitectureFlow",
        "props": {
          "title": "What is an Operator?",
          "steps": [
            {
              "title": "Operands",
              "desc": "The values like 10 and 20"
            },
            {
              "title": "Operator",
              "desc": "The symbol like +"
            },
            {
              "title": "Expression",
              "desc": "The whole combination: 10 + 20"
            }
          ],
          "activeStepIndex": 1
        },
        "popups": [
          {
            "anchor": "calculator",
            "text": "Think of simple math",
            "kind": "tip"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "narration": "Without operators, our variables would just sit there. We need them to calculate discounts, compare ages, or check if a user has enough balance to buy something.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "ComparisonCard",
        "props": {
          "title": "Why we need them",
          "leftTitle": "Variables Alone",
          "leftPoints": [
            "Just store data",
            "Static values",
            "No movement"
          ],
          "rightTitle": "With Operators",
          "rightPoints": [
            "Calculate totals",
            "Compare values",
            "Make decisions"
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "narration": "Let's look at the map of Java operators. We have Arithmetic for math, Relational for comparing, and Logical for combining multiple conditions together.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "DiagramFlow",
        "props": {
          "title": "Operator Categories",
          "nodes": [
            {
              "id": "A",
              "label": "Arithmetic",
              "sub": "+, -, *, /, %"
            },
            {
              "id": "B",
              "label": "Relational",
              "sub": "==, !=, >, <"
            },
            {
              "id": "C",
              "label": "Logical",
              "sub": "&&, ||, !"
            }
          ],
          "edges": [
            {
              "from": "A",
              "to": "B",
              "label": "Flows to"
            },
            {
              "from": "B",
              "to": "C",
              "label": "Flows to"
            }
          ],
          "steps": [
            {
              "at": "Arithmetic",
              "label": "Basic Math",
              "focus": [
                "A"
              ]
            },
            {
              "at": "Relational",
              "label": "Comparisons",
              "focus": [
                "B"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "camera": [
          {
            "at": "map",
            "zoom": 1.0,
            "x": 50,
            "y": 50
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "narration": "Here is the smallest code to add two numbers. We create two variables, add them using the plus operator, and store the result in a third variable.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TypingCode",
        "type_anchor": "Here",
        "props": {
          "filename": "Main.java",
          "language": "java",
          "code": "int apples = 10;\nint bananas = 5;\nint total = apples + bananas;\nSystem.out.println(total);",
          "highlightLines": [
            3
          ]
        },
        "camera": [
          {
            "at": "plus",
            "zoom": 1.7,
            "x": 50,
            "y": 35
          }
        ],
        "lineNotes": [
          {
            "line": 3,
            "text": "The addition happens here"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "narration": "When we run this code, the compiler calculates ten plus five and gives us fifteen. Look at the output in the terminal below.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TerminalRun",
        "props": {
          "command": "java Main.java",
          "output": [
            "15"
          ]
        },
        "camera": [
          {
            "at": "output",
            "zoom": 1.5,
            "x": 50,
            "y": 70
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 8,
      "narration": "Now, let's look at a special operator called modulo. It uses the percent symbol. Instead of giving you the quotient, it gives you the remainder left over.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "StackQueueViz",
        "props": {
          "title": "How Modulo Works",
          "kind": "queue",
          "steps": [
            {
              "at": "special",
              "op": "enqueue",
              "value": "10 / 3",
              "label": "Divide 10 by 3"
            },
            {
              "at": "remainder",
              "op": "enqueue",
              "value": "Rem: 1",
              "label": "Remainder is 1"
            }
          ]
        },
        "popups": [
          {
            "anchor": "percent",
            "text": "% is the Modulo symbol",
            "kind": "note"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 9,
      "narration": "In this code, ten modulo three results in one. This is because three goes into ten three times, leaving a remainder of exactly one.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TypingCode",
        "type_anchor": "In",
        "props": {
          "filename": "Modulo.java",
          "language": "java",
          "code": "int a = 10;\nint b = 3;\nint remainder = a % b;\nSystem.out.println(remainder);",
          "highlightLines": [
            3
          ]
        },
        "camera": [
          {
            "at": "remainder",
            "zoom": 1.8,
            "x": 50,
            "y": 35
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 10,
      "narration": "A common mistake is confusing the single equals sign with the double equals sign. Single equals assigns a value. Double equals checks if two values are the same.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "ComparisonCard",
        "props": {
          "title": "Assignment vs Equality",
          "leftTitle": "=",
          "leftPoints": [
            "Assignment operator",
            "Stores value in variable",
            "Example: x = 5"
          ],
          "rightTitle": "==",
          "rightPoints": [
            "Relational operator",
            "Compares two values",
            "Example: x == 5"
          ]
        },
        "keyIdea": "One sets, two checks"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 11,
      "narration": "Watch out for integer division! If you divide five by two using integers, Java throws away the decimal and gives you two, not two point five.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TypingCode",
        "type_anchor": "Watch",
        "props": {
          "filename": "Mistake.java",
          "language": "java",
          "code": "int x = 5;\nint y = 2;\nint result = x / y;\nSystem.out.println(result);",
          "highlightLines": [
            3
          ]
        },
        "popups": [
          {
            "anchor": "decimal",
            "text": "Integers lose decimals!",
            "kind": "note"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 12,
      "narration": "To fix this, we use the double data type. By using five point zero and two point zero, Java knows to keep the decimal result.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TypingCode",
        "type_anchor": "To",
        "props": {
          "filename": "Fix.java",
          "language": "java",
          "code": "double x = 5.0;\ndouble y = 2.0;\ndouble result = x / y;\nSystem.out.println(result);",
          "highlightLines": [
            3
          ]
        },
        "previous_code": "int x = 5;\nint y = 2;\nint result = x / y;\nSystem.out.println(result);"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 13,
      "narration": "Quick recap. Operators perform actions. Arithmetic is for math. Modulo gives the remainder. And always remember the difference between single and double equals.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "SummaryList",
        "props": {
          "title": "Key Takeaways",
          "items": [
            "Operators are symbols for actions",
            "Modulo (%) returns the remainder",
            "Integer division drops decimals",
            "= is for setting, == is for comparing"
          ]
        },
        "camera": [
          {
            "at": "recap",
            "zoom": 1.0,
            "x": 50,
            "y": 50
          }
        ]
      }
    },
